course: dictionary
lesson: 15893
title: Unjustified: not fair, no good reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unjustified is an adjective. It describes something that is not fair or not reasonable, because there is no good reason behind it. A quick check is to ask yourself, "Was there a good reason for this?" If the answer is no, unjustified may be the right word.
explain: «Sam was told off for something his sister did.» Sam did not do it, so the telling off was unjustified. The word tells you the grown-up had no good reason to blame him.
explain: The start of the word, un-, means not. Justified means fair and based on a good reason, so unjustified means the opposite. Words like unjustified often appear in stories, in news reports and in fairness talks at school.
fact: Unjustified means not fair or not reasonable.
fact: Unjustified means without a good reason.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so unjustified is the opposite of justified.
fact: Phrases to know: «unjustified anger» and «completely unjustified».
fact: A good check is to ask, "Was there a good reason for this?" If not, the word fits.
exercise: «The teacher kept Ali in at break for talking, but Ali had not said a word. The punishment was unjustified.» What does unjustified mean here? | Very long and tiring | *Not fair, with no good reason | Happy and proud | Kind and gentle
exercise: Ruby says unjustified means fair. What is her mistake? | It means fair, with a very good reason | It means fair and kind, and also very funny | *It means the opposite: not fair
exercise: «The news said the claims were completely unjustified.» What does this tell you? | *The claims had no good reason behind them | The claims were very long | The claims were made by a teacher | The claims were all true and proven
test: «The referee gave a penalty for a foul that never happened, so his decision was unjustified.» What does unjustified mean in this sentence? | Loud and angry | *Not fair, with no good reason | Quick and clever | Made in a hurry, but correct
test: Un- at the start of a word means not. What does un- mean in unjustified? | Before | Again | *Not | Very
exam: Which sentence uses unjustified correctly? | «The soup was unjustified and hot.» | «The sunny day was unjustified.» | «She ran unjustified to the shop.» | *«The coach dropped her from the team for no reason at all, and the decision was unjustified.»
